  "metadata": {
    "title": "BYD threatens to sue Trump administration over Chinese military claims",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "BYD has threatened to sue the US government after the Pentagon accused the Chinese carmaker of aiding Beijing's military.\nStella Li, BYD's executive vice-president, said the company would use every legal \"weapon\" at its disposal to fight the claims as she accused the Trump administration of seeking to hobble the Chinese firm because of its growing success internationally.\nThe carmaker recently overtook American rival Tesla to become the world's biggest manufacturer of electric vehicles and has rapidly grown sales in Britain and Europe over the past three years.\nMs Li said: \"Because you are too strong, somebody challenges you – they cannot challenge your products so they will challenge this kind of perception.\n\"But we will use our legal weapons to protect us. Everybody should know in the world: BYD is not a company that can be pushed around, [where] you can give some false claim.\n\"We will use legal action to protect us, protect our reputation.\"\nAsked whether this meant the company intended to sue the US government, she replied: \"We will very openly, transparently talk… But then if everything's getting worse, we have to use our legal protection.\"\nThe pushback comes after the Pentagon included BYD on a list of \"entities identified as Chinese military companies\".\nIn a document published on Monday, the Department of Defence said the company was \"directly and indirectly\" affiliated with SASAC, the institution that manages Chinese state-owned companies, as well as the Chinese ministry of industry and information technology.\nBYD was described as a \"military-civil fusion contributor to the Chinese defence industrial base\", meaning that its technological breakthroughs are being shared with the military to aid modernisation.\nThe blacklisting blocks BYD from securing any US government contracts while signalling to other businesses that it is considered a security threat.\nIt could also pave the way for harsher sanctions later, with the Pentagon saying it \"reserves the right to take additional actions on these entities\".\nE-commerce giant Alibaba and internet search giant Baidu were also among companies included on the list, along with robotics manufacturers RoboSense Technology and Unitree.\nAlibaba and Baidu insist there is no basis for their inclusion and the Pentagon has not provided direct evidence for its claims.\nHowever, American officials argue that the distinction between private and state-owned companies in China is often blurred, while also pointing to Beijing's official \"civil-military fusion\" policies.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "BYD has threatened to sue the US government after the Pentagon accused the Chinese carmaker of aiding Beijing's military.",
      "Stella Li, BYD's executive vice-president, said the company would use every legal \"weapon\" at its disposal to fight the claims as she accused the Trump administration of seeking to hobble the Chinese firm because of its growing success internationally.",
      "The carmaker recently overtook American rival Tesla to become the world's biggest manufacturer of electric vehicles and has rapidly grown sales in Britain and Europe over the past three years.",
      "Ms Li said: \"Because you are too strong, somebody challenges you – they cannot challenge your products so they will challenge this kind of perception.",
      "\"But we will use our legal weapons to protect us. Everybody should know in the world: BYD is not a company that can be pushed around, [where] you can give some false claim.",
      "\"We will use legal action to protect us, protect our reputation.\"",
      "Asked whether this meant the company intended to sue the US government, she replied: \"We will very openly, transparently talk… But then if everything's getting worse, we have to use our legal protection.\"",
      "The pushback comes after the Pentagon included BYD on a list of \"entities identified as Chinese military companies\".",
      "In a document published on Monday, the Department of Defence said the company was \"directly and indirectly\" affiliated with SASAC, the institution that manages Chinese state-owned companies, as well as the Chinese ministry of industry and information technology.",
      "BYD was described as a \"military-civil fusion contributor to the Chinese defence industrial base\", meaning that its technological breakthroughs are being shared with the military to aid modernisation.",
      "The blacklisting blocks BYD from securing any US government contracts while signalling to other businesses that it is considered a security threat.",
      "It could also pave the way for harsher sanctions later, with the Pentagon saying it \"reserves the right to take additional actions on these entities\".",
      "E-commerce giant Alibaba and internet search giant Baidu were also among companies included on the list, along with robotics manufacturers RoboSense Technology and Unitree.",
      "Alibaba and Baidu insist there is no basis for their inclusion and the Pentagon has not provided direct evidence for its claims.",
      "However, American officials argue that the distinction between private and state-owned companies in China is often blurred, while also pointing to Beijing's official \"civil-military fusion\" policies."
